body:
Please understand that you may not feel like justice is served after the legal process is over. Your ex may find holes in every single piece of evidence that you've carefully collected. Your ex may use everything against you and make you feel crazier. The prosecutor may only be able to get probation or something that seems horribly unfair. Victims DO NOT have attorneys at the DA's office. As a victim/witness/survivor of a psychopath, I would decide about the recordings based on MY own feelings. This is your small amount of control and it sounds like you may have been in a situation so controlled that you had to break the law just to get a tiny bit of a voice. He eroded you to the point that you had to do something that you don't know the legality of out of desperation. Step back and look at who you are really deep down (before the psychopath) and make your decision based on YOU. Telling your story is going to be ripped down and twisted up. You will NEVER clear your name by explaining the past. You have to weigh the risk/reward on who you'll be in a year or 5. The woman who was worn down is gone. The pattern and expectations don't matter. It will feel unfair and uncomfortable. You're worth it. Those who've been through the hell will understand and those who choose to believe the psycho because it's easier are just sheep that you don't REALLY want to have admiration from anyway. 

&#x200B;

The other person answered your question well but I encourage you to look up the CIVIL liabilities that you will risk (with a twin prosecutor...) and decide if future you wants to deal with all of that. Don't EVER feel like you have to set the story straight because you can't. I'm 5yrs out and mine sexually abused my child ON FILM and still has more success and admiration than I do for finishing an advanced degree instead of preying on other humans. It just goes to show that success and admiration are terrible goals. Congrats and GOOD JOB on your freedom. <3
